PINFO(2)                       P32W Unix                        PINFO(2)

NAME
       pinfo - read one slot of the process table

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/pinfo.h>

       int pinfo(int slot, struct pinfo *info);

DESCRIPTION
       pinfo copies a summary of process-table slot slot (0 to 63)
       into the struct pinfo at info. P32W has no /dev/kmem, so ps(1),
       top(1) and shutdown(1) read the table this way, a slot at a
       time, until the call fails. <sys/pinfo.h> defines

           struct pinfo {
               int pid, ppid, state, uid, tty, size;
               unsigned time;
               char name[16];
               unsigned ntlb, ncow, nstack;
               unsigned cntlb, cncow, cnstack;
           };

       pid     the process ID; 0 for a free slot
       ppid    the parent's process ID (0 if none)
       state   P_FREE (0), P_EMBRYO (1, being created),
               P_SLEEPING (2), P_RUNNABLE (3), P_RUNNING (4),
               P_ZOMBIE (5), P_STOPPED (6, traced)
       uid     the real user ID
       tty     the controlling terminal's minor number (0 for
               tty0, 1 for tty1), or -1 for none
       size    the break address plus the stack's size, in bytes:
               roughly the memory the process uses
       time    user plus system CPU time, in clock ticks (50 a
               second)
       name    the last component of the program's path name, at
               most 15 characters and a NUL
       ntlb    TLB refills (pages loaded into the TLB on a miss)
       ncow    copy-on-write page copies (after fork)
       nstack  stack pages added
       cntlb, cncow, cnstack
               the same for its children, added when they are
               reaped by wait(2); time -l reports them

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EINVAL  slot is not between 0 and 63.
       EFAULT  info is a bad address.

SEE ALSO
       ps(1), top(1), sysinfo(2)

BUGS
       It is P32W's own: other systems have /proc or /dev/kmem.
